{
 "$comparability": {
  "what": "hand labels, kind: requirement, drawn from eval/results/before-precision-sample.json",
  "labels_remain_valid": true,
  "why": "A label is a judgement about a sentence, not about a build. These verdicts are still judgements about these sentences and node eval/apply-labels.mjs carries them forward, so the labels survive the golden set being rebuilt.",
  "carry_key_changed": "The carry key is now (rfc, kind, text) rather than (rfc, text). One key in this set of files already carried both verdicts - rfc 7872, TRUE as a requirement and FALSE as a candidate - and the old key stored the contradiction silently. apply-labels.mjs now reports it instead of overwriting it."
 },
 "$comment": [
  "Hand labels for eval/results/before-precision-sample.json, items P001-P200, judged",
  "against eval/PRECISION-RUBRIC.md. The whole list is `kind: requirement`: the first",
  "version of the sampler drained the requirement list before touching the candidate",
  "list, so this file measures precision on requirements only. The candidate list has",
  "its own sample, eval/results/before-precision-labels-candidates.json.",
  "",
  "Judged on the text, not on the metadata the tool attached to it. Six of the false",
  "positives are the specification describing its own keywords, five are a list",
  "introducer or a flattened table row, and thirty-one are sentences that are true and",
  "about the protocol rather than binding on an implementer. None of them is a parsing",
  "error; they are the precision cost of a syntactic test."
 ],
